R4-1 - negative-only eligibility test
Concrete example: replace the temporary guard with the earlier code that hides every held warning, then run the new twelve-case eligibility matrix.
Expected under the required review red gate: the new test rejects code lacking the warning correction.
Actual: it reports success, because every case checks that an ineligible warning stays silent.
The immediately following separate eligible-warning test correctly fails: the required warning is hidden.
The same result occurs when the reviewer deliberately adds unconditional held-warning suppression to a temporary current guard.
This is a weakness of one test's oracle; it does not show that the current product is broken.
The combined suite already catches blanket suppression through the separate positive regression.
Location: tests/fm-turnend-guard.test.sh:1734.

Likelihood, impact and cost
The isolated test passes blanket suppression every time; the reviewer reproduced that deterministically.
It hurts maintainers relying on this particular test to prove the latest warning behavior exists.
Current captain-facing behavior remains verified by other positive tests; no lost event or duplicate warning was reproduced in current production.
Reviewer severity is P2 because the required final red gate demands every new behavioral test fail without its fix.
A bounded test-only correction could add an eligible warning control to the matrix before the existing negative cases, preserving production behavior and the accepted race.
Estimated correction cost: 5-10 minutes; next fresh review plus six affected suites: 10-15 minutes; total roughly 15-25 minutes, not a guarantee.
Recommend the bounded test-only correction if continuing, to satisfy the required red gate without more production changes.
The captain may instead decide whether the separate positive regression is sufficient; the worker has not decided or launched a fixer.

